---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FAQSection from '@components/article/FAQSection.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '@components/article/RelatedArticles.astro';

// Article metadata
const title = 'How to Find Reliable WiFi Anywhere in the World (2025)';
const description = 'Proven strategies and tools for finding fast, stable internet while traveling. Stay connected for remote work from cafes, coworking spaces, and hotels worldwide.';
const author = 'FloatJet Team';
const authorUrl = '/about';
const pubDate = new Date('2025-11-23');
const readingTime = 9;
const image = 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1521791136064-7986c2920216?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80';
const imageAlt = 'Person working on laptop in cafe with WiFi';

// Table of contents
const headings = [
  {text: 'Introduction', slug: 'introduction', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Essential Apps & Tools', slug: 'tools', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Best Sources of WiFi', slug: 'sources', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Testing & Troubleshooting', slug: 'testing', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Backup Strategies', slug: 'backups', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Country-Specific Tips', slug: 'country-tips', depth: 2},
  {text: 'FAQ', slug: 'faq', depth: 2},
  {text: 'Conclusion', slug: 'conclusion', depth: 2},
];

// FAQ data
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What WiFi speed do I need for video calls?',
    answer: '<p>For reliable video calls, aim for <strong>10-25 Mbps download and 5-10 Mbps upload</strong>. Zoom recommends 3.8 Mbps for HD group calls, but real-world conditions need more headroom. Always test before important calls. If speeds are borderline, turn off video or use a phone hotspot backup.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Is cafe WiFi safe for work?',
    answer: '<p>Cafe WiFi is convenient but inherently less secure than private networks. <strong>Always use a VPN</strong> on public networks. Avoid accessing sensitive accounts (banking, important admin panels) on public WiFi. For truly sensitive work, use mobile data or a personal hotspot instead.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I know if a hotel has good WiFi before booking?',
    answer: '<p>Check recent reviews specifically mentioning WiFi (filter by "work" or "WiFi" on Booking.com). Workfrom and NomadList have hotel WiFi reports. Message the hotel directly asking for speed test results. Hotels advertising "high-speed internet" often disappoint—look for actual numbers.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'What if there\'s no WiFi at all?',
    answer: '<p>Mobile data is your fallback. Buy a local SIM with data or use eSIM services like Airalo. In areas with poor coverage, Starlink (where legal) or satellite internet cafes may exist. For truly remote locations, plan work around connectivity windows or pre-download what you need.</p>'
  },
  {
    question: 'Should I bring my own router?',
    answer: '<p>Travel routers (like GL.iNet devices) can improve hotel WiFi by boosting signal and adding VPN protection. However, they add weight and complexity. Worth it if you frequently stay in hotels with weak signals; unnecessary if you mostly use coworking spaces or cafes with good connectivity.</p>'
  }
];

// Related articles
const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: 'Essential Travel Tech for Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Compact gadgets and accessories for working from anywhere.',
    href: '/blog/essential-travel-tech-nomads',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1519389950473-47ba0277781c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Travel tech setup'
  },
  {
    title: 'VPN & Public WiFi Security Guide',
    excerpt: 'Protect yourself when working on public networks.',
    href: '/blog/vpn-public-wifi-guide',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563013544-824ae1b704d3?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'WiFi security concept'
  },
  {
    title: 'Top Destinations for Digital Nomads',
    excerpt: 'Cities with great infrastructure for remote work.',
    href: '/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads',
    image: 'https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528181304800-259b08848526?auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80',
    imageAlt: 'Digital nomad destination'
  }
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    author={author}
    authorUrl={authorUrl}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Travel"
    categorySlug="guides/travel"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">The Remote Worker's Constant Challenge</h2>
  <p>
    Your entire livelihood depends on internet connectivity. A dropped video call, a failed file
    upload, or a connection that dies mid-deadline can ruin your day—and your reputation with
    clients.
  </p>
  <p>
    After years of working from cafes, hotels, coworking spaces, and random spots across 40+
    countries, we've developed a system for finding reliable WiFi anywhere. These aren't just
    tips—they're survival strategies for location-independent professionals.
  </p>

  <!-- Essential Apps & Tools -->
  <h2 id="tools">Essential Apps & Tools</h2>

  <h3>WiFi Finder Apps</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>WiFi Map</strong> - Crowdsourced database of passwords and hotspots worldwide</li>
    <li><strong>Wiman</strong> - Free WiFi finder with speed ratings</li>
    <li><strong>Workfrom</strong> - Best for finding work-friendly cafes (includes speed tests)</li>
    <li><strong>NomadList</strong> - City-level WiFi quality and specific venue recommendations</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Speed Testing</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fast.com</strong> - Netflix's speed test, simple and reliable</li>
    <li><strong>Speedtest by Ookla</strong> - More detailed metrics including latency</li>
    <li><strong>Tip:</strong> Test multiple times at different hours—WiFi often degrades during peak times</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Connectivity Backup</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Airalo/Holafly</strong> - eSIM apps for instant mobile data in 100+ countries</li>
    <li><strong>Skyroam/Solis</strong> - Dedicated travel hotspot devices</li>
    <li><strong>Your phone</strong> - Always have mobile data as a backup hotspot</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Best Sources of WiFi -->
  <h2 id="sources">Best Sources of WiFi (Ranked by Reliability)</h2>

  <h3>1. Coworking Spaces (Most Reliable)</h3>
  <p>
    Coworking spaces exist specifically for work, so fast, reliable internet is their core product.
    Most offer day passes if you don't want a monthly membership.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Expect:</strong> 50-200+ Mbps, dedicated work environment, backup connections</li>
    <li><strong>Find them:</strong> Coworker.com, Deskhop, local Google Maps search</li>
    <li><strong>Cost:</strong> $5-25/day or $100-300/month depending on location</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Hotel Business Centers / Lobbies</h3>
  <p>
    Even if your hotel room WiFi is terrible, the lobby or business center often has better,
    more stable connections. Many upscale hotels offer dedicated work areas.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ask for the business center password even if you're not staying there</li>
    <li><strong>Chains with reliable WiFi:</strong> Marriott, Hilton, IHG properties</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Coffee Shops (Variable)</h3>
  <p>
    The classic nomad spot. Quality varies wildly—some cafes have fiber, others have unusable connections.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Check first:</strong> Use Workfrom or Google reviews filtered by "WiFi"</li>
    <li><strong>Best chains:</strong> Starbucks (consistent globally), local specialty coffee shops</li>
    <li><strong>Warning:</strong> Popular spots get crowded and slow during peak hours</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Libraries</h3>
  <p>
    Underrated option. Public libraries typically have free, fast WiFi and a quiet work environment.
    University libraries are even better if you can access them.
  </p>

  <h3>5. Airports</h3>
  <p>
    Airport WiFi has improved dramatically. Many airports now offer free, fast connections. Great
    for catching up on work during layovers.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Best airports for WiFi:</strong> Singapore Changi, Seoul Incheon, most European airports</li>
    <li><strong>Worst:</strong> Many US airports (limited free time), some developing country airports</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>6. Hotel Room WiFi (Often Unreliable)</h3>
  <p>
    Hotel room WiFi is frequently the worst option—congested, slow, and sometimes charged extra.
    Always test before relying on it for important work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Improve it:</strong> Request a room near the router, ask for ethernet cable, use a travel router</li>
    <li><strong>Alternative:</strong> Work from lobby instead of room</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Testing & Troubleshooting -->
  <h2 id="testing">Testing & Troubleshooting</h2>

  <h3>Before Important Work</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Run speed test (need 10+ Mbps download, 5+ upload for video calls)</li>
    <li>Check latency/ping (under 100ms is acceptable, under 50ms is good)</li>
    <li>Test your VPN connection</li>
    <li>Do a quick video call test if possible</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>When WiFi is Slow</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Switch bands:</strong> Try 5GHz instead of 2.4GHz if available</li>
    <li><strong>Move closer:</strong> Physical proximity to router matters</li>
    <li><strong>Off-peak hours:</strong> Test at different times; lunch hours are often congested</li>
    <li><strong>Ask staff:</strong> They may have a staff network or know better spots</li>
    <li><strong>Clear cache:</strong> Sometimes the captive portal is the bottleneck</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When WiFi Cuts Out</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Have backup ready:</strong> Mobile hotspot should be configured and ready</li>
    <li><strong>Save work constantly:</strong> Use auto-save tools, work in cloud apps</li>
    <li><strong>Communicate early:</strong> If connection is unstable, warn call participants</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Backup Strategies -->
  <h2 id="backups">Backup Strategies</h2>

  <h3>Mobile Data as Primary Backup</h3>
  <p>
    Your phone is your emergency internet. Always have data available.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>eSIM:</strong> Airalo, Holafly—download data plans instantly when you land</li>
    <li><strong>Local SIM:</strong> Often cheaper for long stays, buy at airport on arrival</li>
    <li><strong>Hotspot device:</strong> Better battery and multiple device support than phone tethering</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Multiple Location Strategy</h3>
  <p>
    Scout 2-3 reliable work spots in any city you stay in:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Primary: Your go-to coworking or cafe</li>
    <li>Backup 1: Alternative with different internet provider</li>
    <li>Backup 2: Hotel lobby or library</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Offline Work Capability</h3>
  <p>
    Download what you can work on offline:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Sync documents for offline access (Google Docs, Notion)</li>
    <li>Clone git repos locally</li>
    <li>Download reference materials</li>
    <li>Have offline tasks ready for connectivity gaps</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- Country-Specific Tips -->
  <h2 id="country-tips">Country-Specific Tips</h2>

  <h3>Southeast Asia</h3>
  <p>
    Generally excellent connectivity, especially in major cities. Thailand and Vietnam have
    widespread 4G/5G with cheap data plans. Indonesia (outside Bali) can be spotty.
  </p>

  <h3>Latin America</h3>
  <p>
    Improving rapidly. Mexico, Colombia, and Argentina have good urban connectivity. Fiber is
    common in nomad hotspots. Rural areas remain challenging.
  </p>

  <h3>Europe</h3>
  <p>
    Excellent infrastructure throughout. EU roaming makes mobile data easy across borders.
    Eastern Europe often has faster, cheaper internet than Western Europe.
  </p>

  <h3>Africa</h3>
  <p>
    Variable. South Africa, Kenya, and Morocco have decent connectivity. Load shedding in
    South Africa affects everything. Mobile data often more reliable than fixed WiFi.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <!-- Conclusion -->
  <h2 id="conclusion">Stay Connected, Stay Productive</h2>
  <p>
    The key to reliable connectivity while traveling:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research before arrival:</strong> Use apps to find tested, reliable spots</li>
    <li><strong>Always have backup:</strong> Mobile data ready to go</li>
    <li><strong>Test before committing:</strong> Speed test before important calls</li>
    <li><strong>Scout multiple locations:</strong> Never rely on a single spot</li>
    <li><strong>Protect yourself:</strong> Use a VPN on all public networks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Internet reliability has improved dramatically worldwide. With the right preparation and
    tools, you can work productively from almost anywhere. The occasional connectivity hiccup
    is the price of freedom—and it's getting rarer every year.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before working on public WiFi, make sure you understand
    <a href="/blog/vpn-public-wifi-guide">WiFi security best practices</a> and have a good
    <a href="/tools/best-vpn-digital-nomads">VPN installed</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
